[R0449] year=2026 | venue=4th Cognitive Models and Artificial Intelligence Conference, AICCONF 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AI-Driven Security Operations Centers: A Research Landscape Analysis
KEYWORDS: Agent Based Systems; Artificial Intelligence; Human in the Loop; Large Language Models (LLMs); Security Automation; Security Operations Center (SOC); Architecture; Automation; Autonomous agents; Computational methods; Decision support systems; Intelligent agents; Security systems; Agent-based approach; Agent-based systems; Human-in-the-loop; Landscape analysis; Large language model; Model based approach; Security automation; Security operation center; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Security Operations Centers (SOCs) are leveraging AI, large language models and agent-based approaches as the alert volume and operational complexity grows. This research systematically maps and analyzes AI enable SOC research in terms of its application area, level of automation, models and architecture deployed, evaluation practice, security risk, and human - AI interaction design. The findings indicated that most of the applications of artificial intelligence systems are in log analysis, alert triage, threat intelligence processing, incident response and analyst decision support. The SOC workflows are evolving toward semi-autonomous architectures supported by AI and integrated with SOAR, but most implementations remain human in the loop or human on the loop, while fully autonomous SOCs are mostly conceptual. Performance evaluations are primarily based on operational and technical metrics, such as MTTD, MTTR, precision, recall and false positives. The literature also identifies challenges such as explainability limitations, hallucination risks, integration complexity and excessive reliance on automation. Overall, this study offers orderly perspectives on current AI-enabled SOCs. Furthermore, it also highlights the need for human-centered, explainable and trustworthy AI designs for future SOC architectures. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0450] year=2026 | venue=Communications in Computer and Information Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Automatic Assessment of Node Compromise Risks in Critical Infrastructures via a Multi-stage Uncertainty Reduction Model Using LLMs
KEYWORDS: CVE; CVSS; Cybersecurity Automation; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Artificial intelligence; Critical infrastructures; Cybersecurity; Network security; Public works; Risk perception; Uncertainty analysis; Automatic assessment; CVE; CVSS; Cyber security; Cybersecurity automation; Language model; Large language model; Multi-stages; Node compromise; Penetration testing; Risk assessment
ABSTRACT: The security of Critical Infrastructures is increasingly threatened by the complexity of heterogeneous network devices. Although penetration testing remains the gold standard for verifying device security, the inherent limitation derived from the need for human operators to perform testing makes this activity resource-intensive and unscalable. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a fully automated four-stage framework for assessing the Node Compromise Risk. The proposed architecture operates as a progressive pipeline, starting from lightweight vulnerability mapping to resource-intensive verification phases based on Generative AI. At each stage, a dynamic metric updates the estimated risk, allowing the system to stop the analysis for safe nodes and allocate computational resources solely to high-risk targets. Generative AI is used in the final stage to dynamically adapt raw Proof-of-Concept exploits to the specific target configuration, bridging the gap between theoretical vulnerability and verified compromise. Experimental results conducted in a controlled environment demonstrate the framework’s ability to autonomously identify, filter, and exploit vulnerabilities, effectively reducing uncertainty about the security posture of a node. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0451] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE Conference on Artificial Intelligence, CAI 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Large Language Models are Autonomous Cyber Defenders
KEYWORDS: Ai Agents; Autonomous Cyber Defense; Incident Response; Large Language Models; Reinforcement Learning; Adversarial machine learning; Autonomous agents; Computational methods; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Systems analysis; Ai agent; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber-defense; Effective incident response; Incident response; Language model; Large language model; Model learning; Multi agent; Reinforcement learnings; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: Fast and effective incident response is essential to prevent adversarial cyberattacks. Autonomous Cyber Defense (ACD) aims to automate incident response through Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents that plan and execute actions. Most ACD approaches focus on single-agent scenarios and leverage Reinforcement Learning (RL). However, ACD RL-trained agents depend on costly training, and their reasoning is not always explainable or transferable. Large Language Models (LLMs) can address these concerns by providing explainable actions in general security contexts. Researchers have explored LLM agents for ACD but have not evaluated them on multi-agent scenarios or interacting with other ACD agents. In this paper, we show the first study on how LLMs perform in multi-agent ACD environments by proposing a new integration to the CybORG CAGE 4 environment. We examine how ACD teams of LLM and RL agents can interact by proposing a novel communication protocol. Our results highlight the strengths and weaknesses of LLMs and RL and help us identify promising research directions to create, train, and deploy future teams of ACD agents. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0452] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 22nd International Conference on Distributed Computing in Smart Systems and the Internet of Things, DCOSS-IoT 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: CyberAId: AI-Driven Cybersecurity for Financial Service Providers
KEYWORDS: cyberaid; digital twins; DORA; financial cybersecurity; LLM agents; multi-agent systems; retrieval-augmented generation; Authentication; Autonomous agents; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; Digital twin; Electronic money; Finance; Intelligent agents; Intrusion detection; Network security; Nickel compounds; Cyber security; Cyberaid; DORA; Financial cybersecurity; Financial institution; Financial services providers; LLM agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Regulatory pressure; Retrieval-augmented generation; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: European financial institutions face mounting regulatory pressure under DORA, NIS2 and the EU AI Act while their security operations centres remain constrained not by data or staffing but by reasoning capacity: enterprise SIEMs cover only a fraction of MITRE ATT&CK techniques, two thirds of SOC teams cannot keep pace with alert volumes, and the majority of breaches are preceded by alerts that are generated but never investigated. Frontier large language models now achieve state-of-the-art results on isolated cybersecurity tasks - one-day vulnerability exploitation, code-level patching, intrusion detection - yet no narrow win constitutes a platform that can compose across functions, persist multi-tenant state, map findings to regulatory regimes and survive an audit. This position paper argues that the right unit of construction is a hybrid multi-agent system in which specialised LLM subagents reason over classical SIEM/XDR telemetry rather than replacing it, share accumulated agent state across institutions through privacy-preserving federation, and can connect to complementary capability packs such as quantum-based authentication, digital twins for adversarial validation, and eBPF-based kernel telemetry. We present CyberAId, a model-agnostic, on-premise-deployable platform in which a Main Agent/CRA coordination layer, a Reporting capability, and specialist subagents operate within a shared runtime under bounded human-in-the-loop autonomy, organised around four falsifiable design principles, and aligned with DORA reporting timelines and the EU AI Act. CyberAId will be validated at four representative financial use cases - client impersonation, anti-money-laundering for payment service providers, retail-banking incident response, and high-frequency-trading resilience - and propose skill-based agent adaptation as the most promising research direction for turning each deployment into a contribution to a continuously refined collective defence. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0453] year=2026 | venue=Communications in Computer and Information Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Leveraging Generative AI for Proactive Security and Automated Remediation in Cloud-Native CI/CD Pipelines
KEYWORDS: Automated Remediation; Cloud-Native; DevSecOps; Explainable AI; Generative AI; Proactive Security; Artificial intelligence; Automation; Cloud security; Information systems; Network security; Remediation; Automated remediation; Cloud-native; Detection accuracy; Devsecop; Embeddings; Explainable AI; Generative AI; Proactive security; Security challenges; Security controls; Pipelines
ABSTRACT: Modern cloud-native CI/CD pipelines enable rapid software delivery but introduce new security challenges. We propose GenSecAI-Ops, a framework embedding specialized generative AI agents across the pipeline for proactive security and automated remediation. GenSecAI-Ops uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and eXplainable AI (XAI) for reliable security automation. Implemented using GitHub Actions and Jenkins, our framework demonstrates significant improvements in detection accuracy, remediation correctness, and developer trust while maintaining pipeline velocity. The approach provides a practical blueprint for integrating AI-driven security controls in DevSecOps workflows. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0454] year=2025 | venue=2nd International Conference on Electronics, Computing, Communication and Control Technology, ICECCC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Prompt Injection in Large Language Model Exploitation: A Security Perspective
KEYWORDS: Adversarial Attacks; Ethical AI; Large Language Models; Open-source AI; Prompt Injection; Prompt Security; Security Vulnerability; Threat Detection; Cybersecurity; Network security; Ethical AI; Language model; Large language model; Open-source; Open-source AI; Prompt injection; Prompt security; Rapid growth; Security vulnerabilities; Threat detection; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: With the rapid growth of AI technologies, ensuring the security of open-source Large Language Models (LLMs) is crucial to maintaining their reliability and trustworthiness. The paper presents a detailed framework to evaluate the security risks of these models in today's fast-changing technological world. This framework includes generators, probes, detectors, and evaluation methods, all centered around the Prompt Inject framework, which helps identify vulnerabilities and possible attacks on LLMs. By using this approach, organizations, researchers, and developers can assess how easily these models can be manipulated and take steps to improve their security. The paper highlights important applications like security testing, penetration testing, compliance checks, and continuous monitoring, ensuring that LLMs remain safe and reliable. This research is valuable for strengthening cybersecurity efforts in the era of open-source AI, helping to build safer and more trustworthy AI systems. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0455] year=2025 | venue=2025 2nd International Conference on Algorithms, Software Engineering and Network Security, ASENS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Intelligent Cybersecurity Defense Strategies Based on Large Language Model Agents
KEYWORDS: Agent Systems; Attack Detection; Cybersecurity; Intelligent Defense; Large Language Models; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Intelligent agents; Network security; Agent systems; Attack detection; Comparative analyzes; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Defense strategy; Intelligent defense; Language model; Large language model; Model agents; Agents
ABSTRACT: As the complexity of cyberattacks continues to increase, traditional static defense strategies have shown significant limitations. This study proposes an intelligent cybersecurity defense framework based on large language model Agents, and conducts a comparative analysis of the effectiveness of various leading models in the cybersecurity defense domain. By developing an attack behavior model, designing an Agent decision-making mechanism, and constructing a defense strategy execution and evaluation system, we assess the defense performance of models such as GPT-4.5, Claude 3.7, LLaMA 3.3, and Gemini 2.0 under different attack scenarios. The experimental results demonstrate that defense strategies based on LLM Agents outperform traditional methods in terms of attack detection rate, response time, and false alarm rate. Furthermore, each model exhibits unique advantages in specific security contexts. This research provides new insights for the development of more adaptive and efficient cybersecurity defense systems. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0456] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Perses: Unlocking Privilege Escalation for Small LLMs via Extensible Heterogeneity
KEYWORDS: Automatic Algorithm Configuration; Cybersecurity Automation; LLMs; Penetration Testing; Vulnerability Detection; Human engineering; Algorithm configurations; Automatic algorithm configuration; Automatic algorithms; Cyber security; Cybersecurity automation; Language model; LLM; Misconfigurations; Penetration testing; Vulnerability detection; Cybersecurity
ABSTRACT: Misconfiguration poses a ubiquitous security vulnerability for even modern hardened systems. Recent advances in Large Language Model (LLM)-based penetration testing have proven its efficacy in vulnerability detection. Prior research has predominantly focused on large, non-local models, evaluating them exclusively on Linux and Windows systems. We developed Perses, an extensible multi-LLM framework based on heterogeneity in model selection and task decomposition, designed to enable small local models to autonomously detect and exploit misconfiguration vulnerabilities. Perses is evaluated on a FreeBSD port of an existing privilege escalation benchmark, analyzing the effects of heterogeneity, the viability of automatic LLM assignment, the criticality of introduced LLM-roles, and the detriment incurred through evaluation on a system the models have little inherent knowledge on. Experimental results reveal that Perses, in its default configuration, enables Small Models to consistently exploit 87.5% of the evaluated vulnerabilities, significantly improving upon the 12.5% reported in prior work. We further find that SMAC-based automatic configuration is feasible, each role is essential for Perses' performance, and that the models contain sufficient knowledge on FreeBSD. These results showcase the power of Small Models in privilege escalation, presenting a viable alternative to Large Models. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0457] year=2024 | venue=Proceedings of the 2024 IEEE International Conference on Cyber Security and Resilience, CSR 2024 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Cyberattack with Security-Augmented Generative Artificial Intelligence
KEYWORDS: Attacks and Defense; Cybersecurity; Generative Artificial Intelligence; Hacking; Large Language Models; Low-Rank Adaptation; Machine Learning; Penetration Testing; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Vulnerability; Adversarial machine learning; Generative adversarial networks; Model checking; Zero-day attack; Attack and defense; Cyber security; Generative artificial intelligence; Hacking; Language model; Large language model; Low-rank adaptation; Machine-learning; Penetration testing; Retrieval-augmented generation; Vulnerability; Cyber attacks
ABSTRACT: Ethical hacking and penetration testing is a vital task by cybersecurity professionals to find and exploit possible vulnerabilities in a system before malicious actors do. However, system hacking has a high barrier to entry that necessitates years of experiential learning and formal education. The rapid development of generative artificial intelligence (AI) may potentially lower the barrier to entry. This research experiments with automatic penetration testing via large language models (LLMs) augmented with security information. This research uses a locally hosted Mistral 7B model with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to improve penetration testing. When the LLMs are fine tuned with limited and unstructured security data such as privilege escalation articles from a few public web sites, the system succeeds in achieving privilege escalation on Linux hosts. The results of this research suggest that no-cost LLM-assisted penetration testing is possible even on ordinary PCs using locally hosted models. Future research is needed to achieve more diversified attacks and discover zero-day vulnerabilities, perhaps with better prompt engineering, models, and security data. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0458] year=2026 | venue=7th International Conference on Computational Vision and Bio Inspired Computing, ICCVBIC 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: An Explainable AI-Based Unified Vendor Risk Assessment System
KEYWORDS: Compliance Document Analysis; Cybersecurity Risk Assessment; Document Intelligence; Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI); Large Language Models (LLMs); Natural Language Processing (NLP); Vendor Risk Management; Artificial intelligence; Automation; Decision making; Information retrieval; Learning systems; Natural language processing systems; Risk analysis; Risk assessment; Compliance document analyze; Cyber security; Cybersecurity risk assessment; Document intelligence; Documents analysis; Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI); Language model; Language processing; Large language model; Natural language processing; Natural languages; Risks assessments; Risks management; Vendor risk management; Risk management
ABSTRACT: Transforming security assessments of third-party vendors with Explainable Artificial Intelligence(XAI) and intelligent document analysis, the paper suggests an automated Unified Risk Assessment System to analyze SOC reports, vendor questionnaires, and penetration testing reports to produce a holistic and clarifiable third-party vendor risk assessment. Manual analysis of complex compliance documents makes up the traditional vendor risk management processes, and is time-intensive, subject to human error and inaccurate across reviewers. The currently available automated systems are mainly specialized in basic data extraction and do not have the ability to take into consideration context or recognize exceptions. The system in question can fill these gaps by ingesting, processing and analyzing a number of security artifacts in real time. It uses state of the art PDF table extractors which use rule-based heuristics in conjunction with machine learning column-detecting capabilities to accurately break down complex compliance files. Moreover, the system applies Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Large Language Models (LLMs) to identify compliance exceptions, interpret security findings, and generate explainable, context-aware risk justifications. The system also calculates dynamic vendor risk scores through adaptive threshold based classification to categorize risk more accurately in different situations of data completeness. Using SOC reports, penetration testing, and vendor questionnaires as input data, the system yields a vendor risk posture with holistic, explainable and human-understandable risk justifications that improve transparency and support decision-making. The strategy accessible to the use of this article saves a lot of time as well as effort which was previously taken up by hand during the assessment of third-party vendors. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0459] year=2026 | venue=Algorithms | type=Article
TITLE: Efficient Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing in Automotive Engineering
KEYWORDS: cybersecurity in automotive; Large Language Models; penetration testing; Retrieval Augmented Generation; vulnerability assessment; Benchmarking; Cost effectiveness; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; Information retrieval; Knowledge based systems; Open systems; Automotives; Cost effective; Cyber security; Cybersecurity in automotive; Language model; Large language model; Open-source; Penetration testing; Retrieval augmented generation; Vulnerability assessments; Automotive engineering
ABSTRACT: Hundreds of connected components expose vehicle systems to an increasing number of cyber attacks. Vehicle manufacturers must establish the most appropriate procedures for vulnerability assessment and penetration testing using a mix of proprietary solutions and open-source standards. The spread of Large Language Models (LLMs) simplifies the interaction between automotive experts and domain-specific knowledge bases. While proprietary LLM services can be expensive and raise data privacy concerns, open-source LLMs are potentially more cost-effective and better suited to in-house solutions. However, the effectiveness of open-source models in retrieving automotive-related cybersecurity information remains unclear. While adopting open-source LLMs with a few billion parameters, their reasoning and generative capabilities under in-context learning settings are questionable. To bridge this gap, this paper explores efficient solutions for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture for automotive cybersecurity relying on open-source LLMs. The ultimate goal is to enable cost-effective retrieval and question answering from in-domain knowledge bases, overcoming the privacy and confidentiality issues raised by automotive experts. Using a Graph Knowledge Base designed for a corporate scenario, this paper first defines an expert-curated testing benchmark to evaluate in-domain question-answering performance across multiple aspects. Next, it proposes different RAG system variants based on various retrieval strategies and LLMs, both proprietary and open-source. Finally, it quantitatively evaluates the effectiveness of the content retrieval strategies and compares the pertinence, conciseness, and completeness of generated answers through human validation. Notably, within the scope of the performed analysis, RAGs that rely on open-source models demonstrate promising and competitive performance in some respects compared to the OpenAI GPT model. RAG retrieval performance also surpasses that of state-of-the-art solutions on existing cybersecurity benchmarks (Recall@K above 0.95 vs. 0.65 for state-of-the-art in-domain RAGs). © 2026 by the authors.

[R0460] year=2025 | venue=Cluster Computing | type=Article
TITLE: Leveraging Intra- and Inter-References in vulnerability detection using Multi-Agent collaboration based on LLMs
KEYWORDS: Code vulnerabilities; Inter-references; Intra-references; Large language models; Multi-agent approach; Retrieval-augmented generation; Codes (symbols); Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Code vulnerability; Inter-reference; Intra-reference; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Multi-agent approach; Multi-turn; Retrieval-augmented generation; Vulnerability detection; Computational methods
ABSTRACT: As AI technology advances, early detection of code vulnerabilities becomes increasingly critical for preventing exploitation, reducing remediation costs, enhancing user trust, and improving system performance. Recently, Large language Models (LLMs) have shown remarkable performance across various tasks using few-shot learning. This study aims to adopt LLM-based agents in a multi-turn discussion framework enhanced with a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) strategy to improve response quality. Our multi-agent approach transforms a single LLM into a highly collaborative intelligence through multi-turn self-collaboration with diverse personas. By leveraging their combined expertise, this approach enhances the accuracy of code vulnerability detection and improves inference capabilities. To effectively utilize few-shot learning samples, we employ intra-references to extract a small number of similar samples from the training data, and inter-references to obtain samples from external data. The experimental studies are conducted on the TreeVul_Ext dataset, and our model achieves promising results across different evaluation criteria. Furthermore, we apply our model to detect code vulnerabilities in low-resource languages and exhibit competitive performance as well. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2025.

[R0461] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes of the Institute for Computer Sciences, Social-Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering, LNICST | type=Conference paper
TITLE: GPT Promotes Intelligent Autonomy in Communication Networks
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Networks; GPT; Large Language Models; Network Intelligence; Anomaly detection; Computer system recovery; Data privacy; Intelligent networks; Mobile telecommunication systems; Network management; Network security; Optimization; Autonomous networks; Communications networks; Generative pre-trained transformer; Intelligent autonomy; Language model; Large language model; Mobile communication technology; Network demands; Network intelligence; Networks management; Site selection
ABSTRACT: With the continuous progress of mobile communication technology and the continuous growth of network demand, the network structure is becoming increasingly complex. However, traditional network management is difficult to meet the needs of future development. In the future, intelligent autonomous networks could perform flexibly and efficiently with the help of AI-driven automated analysis and multidimensional data perception. Still, at the same time, this also requires a more intelligent approach to network management. The large language models (LLMs) represented by generative pre-trained transformer (GPT) will play an important role in promoting intelligent autonomy of communication networks. Therefore, this paper studies the specific methods of GPT promoting intelligent autonomy of communication networks, and analyzes how GPT enables intelligent autonomy in communication networks from different perspectives. Specifically, it includes GPT-assisted base station site selection, antenna design optimization and virtualized intelligent slicing, as well as network operations and maintenance from anomaly detection to automatic recovery, and network traffic optimization, coverage optimization and signaling tracing. Finally, we also propose some challenges, such as the inconsistent quality of training data sets, insufficient computing resources, and high risks to network privacy and security. We also propose some corresponding solutions and predict future development trends. © ICST Institute for Computer Sciences, Social Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering 2026.

[R0462] year=2026 | venue=2026 4th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Applications: Healthcare and Internet of Things, AIMLA 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Full Stack E-Commerce System With Role-Based Access, Integrated AI Chatbot, and NLP-Driven Product Search
KEYWORDS: AI Chatbot; E-Commerce; Full-Stack Development; Gemini AI; JWT Authentication; NLP; PostgreSQL; Product Ranking; RESTful API; Stripe; TF-IDF; Application programming interfaces (API); Authentication; Chatbots; Cryptography; Information management; Information systems; Integration; Malware; Metadata; Network security; Product design; Query languages; Query processing; Relational database systems; Search engines; User experience; Websites; AI chatbot; Chatbots; Full-stack development; Geminus AI; JSON web token authentication; PostgreSQL; Product ranking; RESTful API; Stripe; TF-IDF; Electronic commerce
ABSTRACT: The paper discusses the design and implementation of a full stack e-commerce website powered by AI search for product recommendations for great user experience, built using an altered MERN stack which uses PostgreSQL as its database instead of Mongo DB. It has a 2-stage NLP driven AI powered product search using integration of Google’s Gemini API and an integrated AI chatbot module which is also powered by the Gemini’s LLM (Large Language Model) which assists users, recommends products based on categories, has a recording of user satisfaction score of 4.3 out of 5.0. The mean response time of the query is 320 ms, top 3 product recommendation relevance accuracy rate of 88.6% over a test set of 500 queries. This platform uses PostgreSQL, which is a relational database management system, enhances the complex e-commerce scenarios of real digital realm. The project uses React.js for frontend development and Node.js and Express.js for backend development along with Gemini API integration for product recommendations and smart product search. And uses stripe for secure payment backend processing, cloudinary for media management. The system features ai integrated product search, product filtering based on price range, categories, reviews, new arrivals, hot deals and highly rated products. JSON web tokens (JWT) is used for secure user authentication, stateless authentication, bcrypt password hashing (cost factor 12) parameterized SQL queries help to prevent malware injections and an admin dashboard that is designed to showcase real time insights and analytics of the composed website, includes statistics, total revenue earned based on each month, users, products and orders metadata. This paper extracts the architecture and implementation of the proposed system and the deployment of the platform with relational databases and AI features for a user-friendly and scalable e-commerce platform. The paper demonstrates the integration of payment modules with webhooks, password hashing, JWT user authentication and AI-driven search functions for product recommendations. The payment module of this project is integrated with Stripe webhook. These security measures comprehensively reduce the security breaches and attacks by 97.4% in penetration testing. Empirical results confirm that the proposed platform surpasses the traditional keyword search architectures. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0463] year=2026 | venue=2026 Systems and Information Engineering Design Symposium, SIEDS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Systematic Capability Benchmarking of Frontier Large Language Models for Offensive Cyber Tasks
KEYWORDS: capture the flag; cybersecurity benchmarking; large language models; LLM evaluation; multi-agent systems; offensive security; penetration testing; Application programming interfaces (API); Benchmarking; Cost effectiveness; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Linux; Network security; Capture the flag; Cyber security; Cybersecurity benchmarking; Language model; Large language model; LLM evaluation; Multiagent systems (MASs); Offensive security; Penetration testing; Performance; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: We present, to our knowledge, the most comprehensive cross-model evaluation of LLM agents on offensive cybersecurity tasks, benchmarking 10 frontier models from 7 providers on all 200 challenges of the NYU CTF Bench. Building on the D-CIPHER multi-agent framework, we extend it with multi-provider backend support, a custom Kali Linux environment with over 100 pre-installed penetration testing tools, and runtime tool-discovery agents. Through a controlled factorial study, we find that the Kali Linux environment yields a +9.5 percentage-point improvement over Ubuntu, while auto-prompting and category-specific tips often degrade performance in well-equipped environments. Among models, Claude 4.5 Opus achieves the highest solve rate (59%), followed by Gemini 3 Pro (52%), with Gemini 3 Flash offering the best cost-efficiency at $0.05 per solve. Asymmetric planner/executor model assignments provide no meaningful benefit while coherent same-model configurations consistently outperform mixed-tier pairings. Our results indicate that environment tooling and model selection emerge as the strongest drivers of performance, whereas prompt engineering interventions show diminishing or negative returns in well-equipped environments. Reported performance reflects both model reasoning ability and compatibility with agent tooling and API integration. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0464] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 12th International Conference on Network Softwarization: Autonomous and Reliable Softwarized Networks in the Age of Distributed Intelligence, NetSoft 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Hybrid Neuro-Symbolic Architecture for Autonomous Intrusion Detection and Mitigation in 5G and beyond Networks
KEYWORDS: Artificial Intelligence; Beyond- Fifth Generation; cybersecurity; Neuro-symbolic AI; Computation theory; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Deep learning; Intrusion detection; Learning systems; Memory architecture; Network architecture; Network intrusion; Zero-day attack; Beyond- fifth generation; Cognitive theory; Cyber security; Dual process; High Speed; Intrusion-Detection; Learning architectures; Monolithics; Neuro-symbolic AI; Zero day attack; Next generation networks
ABSTRACT: The dynamic and heterogeneous nature of 5G and beyond (B5G) networks creates fertile ground for sophisticated Zero-Day attacks that evade traditional, signature-based defences. This paper presents a novel approach that utilizes a bifurcated, hybrid neuro-symbolic framework - conceptually aligned with dual-process cognitive theory - for autonomous intrusion detection and mitigation from context-rich flow telemetry. By deploying a computationally asymmetric pipeline, our method avoids reliance on monolithic deep learning architectures and uses a high-speed, confidence-gated XGBoost classifier (System 1) to evaluate traffic at wirespeed and instantly discover anomalous deviations. Evaluations demonstrate strong performance in managing out-of-distribution traffic and detecting previously unknown threats, offering a scalable, highly reliable, and interpretable solution for next-generation network security. The tactical mechanism is dynamically linked to a Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Model (System 2) that provides deep semantic reasoning concerning ambiguous anomalies and synthesizes highly targeted JSON mitigation payloads, which are strictly bounded by deterministic mathematical guardrails to suppress AI hallucination. © 2026 IEEE.

