TITLE: LLM-powered threat intelligence: Proactive detection of zero-day attacks in electric vehicle cyber-physical systems
KEYWORDS: Automotive cybersecurity; Cyber-physical systems; Electric vehicles; Generative AI; Intrusion detection; Large language models; Zero-day attacks; Anomaly detection; Control systems; Cybersecurity; Electric machine control; Electric vehicles; Embedded systems; Malware; Vehicle detection; Vehicle to vehicle communications; Zero-day attack; Automotive cybersecurity; Automotives; Cybe-physical systems; Cyber security; Cyber-physical systems; Generative AI; Intrusion-Detection; Language model; Large language model; Zero day attack; Cyber Physical System; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: Context: Modern electric vehicles (EVs) are sophisticated cyber-physical systems (CPSs) comprising interconnected electronic control units (ECU), sensors, and communication interfaces. This connectivity enhances functionality while expanding the attack surface, exposing EVs to cyber threats. Problem: Zero-day attacks on EV systems pose severe risks by exploiting unknown vulnerabilities and bypassing traditional signature-based intrusion detection systems (IDS). Conventional detection mechanisms struggle with novel attack patterns and dynamic EV environments. Solution: We propose a generative AI-driven framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to proactively identify zero-day attacks in EV ecosystems. The framework integrates generative threat intelligence with anomaly detection, enabling real-time synthesis and detection of emerging attack vectors. Methods: A curated EV threat intelligence corpus was used to fine-tune an LLM that monitors in-vehicle network traffic and sensor telemetry for anomalies. We evaluate detection capabilities through prompt-based zero-day exploit simulation. Results: The LLM-based system achieves high detection accuracy (>98 %) with significantly reduced false positives compared to classical IDS approaches. It successfully identified complex attack scenarios (charging infrastructure ransomware, sensor spoofing in autonomous systems) that evaded baseline methods. Case studies demonstrate real-time detection with automated mitigation recommendations. Contributions: (1) a novel EV cybersecurity architecture combining LLMs with generative attack simulation; (2) a comprehensive EV attack dataset including synthetic zero-days; (3) extensive evaluation against baseline IDS with performance, latency, and interpretability analysis; (4) insights on deploying generative AI for automotive cybersecurity aligned with industry standards. The approach provides proactive defense for next-generation vehicles. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd

[R0466] year=2026 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Designing an AI-Assisted Cyber Threat Intelligence Framework for Industry 4.0: A Human-in-the-Loop Design Science Approach
KEYWORDS: cyber threat intelligence; Industry 4.0; manufacturing cybersecurity; MITRE ATT&CK; natural language processing; proactive security; social media intelligence; Accident prevention; Autonomous agents; Cybersecurity; Industrial management; Industrial research; Industry 4.0; Intelligent agents; Intelligent control; Man machine systems; Multi agent systems; Network security; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Language processing; Manufacturing cybersecurity; MITRE ATT&CK; Natural language processing; Natural languages; Proactive security; Social media; Social medium intelligence; analytical parameters; architecture; Article; artificial intelligence; behavior assessment; communication protocol; cyber threat intelligence; data accuracy; data analysis; data base; evaluation study; feasibility study; information processing; information technology; large language model; loop design science; nonhuman; qualitative analysis; retrieval augmented generation; science; semi structured interview; social media; telephone interview; thematic analysis; validation study; Natural language processing systems
ABSTRACT: The convergence of Information Technology (IT) and Operational Technology (OT) in Industry 4.0 has intensified the need for timely, trustworthy, and explainable cyber threat intelligence (CTI) for Industrial Control Systems (ICS). However, existing AI-enabled and Large Language Model (LLM)-based CTI solutions are predominantly designed for conventional IT environments and do not adequately address the safety, latency, governance, and operational constraints of industrial settings. This paper presents an AI-assisted CTI framework tailored to ICS and Industry 4.0 environments, integrating multi-source data ingestion, a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) knowledge store, a modular chain-of-agents architecture, and an explicit human-in-the-loop verification gate. Following a Design Science Research approach, the framework was evaluated through expert assessment involving twelve cybersecurity practitioners with experience in industrial and Security Operations Centre (SOC) environments and complemented by a proof-of-concept artefact instantiation based on the APT41 DUST campaign. The prototype integrated five heterogeneous CTI evidence sources and executed the automated analytical workflow in approximately 25 s (25.29 s) while illustrating evidence-grounded retrieval, specialized agent orchestration, and human-supervised intelligence generation. Practitioner feedback indicated that AI-assisted contextual intelligence and agent-based reasoning were perceived as valuable, while successful adoption depends primarily on governance, explainability, trust, and alignment with existing operational workflows rather than algorithmic sophistication alone. The study contributes a design-science artefact that combines retrieval-augmented intelligence, modular AI agents, and human oversight, providing practical design guidance for trustworthy AI-assisted CTI deployment in safety-critical Industry 4.0 environments. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0467] year=2026 | venue=2026 Middle East and North Africa Communications Conference, MENACOMM 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Security Framework for AI-Native 6G Networks: Architecture and Threat Taxonomy
KEYWORDS: AI-Native 6G networks; Autonomous security; LLMs; Multi-agent coordination; Red teaming; Autonomous agents; Memory architecture; Network architecture; Network security; AI-native 6g network; Autonomous security; G-networks; Language model; Large language model; Model-driven; Multi-agent coordinations; Red teaming; Security frameworks; Service management; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Sixth-Generation (6 G) networks are envisioned as AI-native systems, where large language models (LLMs) and generative AI orchestrate intent-based networking, autonomous service management, and dynamic slicing. While these capabilities promise unprecedented efficiency and flexibility, they also introduce novel security challenges, i.e., LLM-driven controllers are vulnerable to intent injection, model inversion, and adversarial manipulation, potentially impacting core, edge, and userplane functions. This paper provides a comprehensive review of the security landscape for LLM-driven 6 G networks, mapping known LLM vulnerabilities to telecom network functions, and analyzing approaches for autonomous detection, mitigation, and self-healing. We examine multi-agent red teaming, zero-touch security frameworks, and AI-powered orchestration (SMO), and discuss alignment with O-RAN and ETSI ZSM (Zero-Touch Service Management) standards. Our review identifies open challenges, research trends, and practical considerations for building resilient, self-defending 6 G networks. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0468] year=2024 | venue=Sensors | type=Article
TITLE: CIPHER: Cybersecurity Intelligent Penetration-Testing Helper for Ethical Researcher
KEYWORDS: AI penetration testing assistant; domain specific LLM; large language model; LLM evaluation; penetration testing; pentesting LLM; vulnerabillity detection; Artificial Intelligence; Computer Security; Humans; Research Personnel; Software; Open source software; AI penetration testing assistant; Domain specific; Domain specific large language model; Language model; Large language model; Large language model evaluation; Model evaluation; Penetration testing; Pentesting large language model; Vulnerabillity detection; artificial intelligence; computer security; human; personnel; software; Model checking
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing, a critical component of cybersecurity, typically requires extensive time and effort to find vulnerabilities. Beginners in this field often benefit from collaborative approaches with the community or experts. To address this, we develop Cybersecurity Intelligent Penetration-testing Helper for Ethical Researchers (CIPHER), a large language model specifically trained to assist in penetration testing tasks as a chatbot. Unlike software development, penetration testing involves domain-specific knowledge that is not widely documented or easily accessible, necessitating a specialized training approach for AI language models. CIPHER was trained using over 300 high-quality write-ups of vulnerable machines, hacking techniques, and documentation of open-source penetration testing tools augmented in an expert response structure. Additionally, we introduced the Findings, Action, Reasoning, and Results (FARR) Flow augmentation, a novel method to augment penetration testing write-ups to establish a fully automated pentesting simulation benchmark tailored for large language models. This approach fills a significant gap in traditional cybersecurity Q&A benchmarks and provides a realistic and rigorous standard for evaluating LLM’s technical knowledge, reasoning capabilities, and practical utility in dynamic penetration testing scenarios. In our assessments, CIPHER achieved the best overall performance in providing accurate suggestion responses compared to other open-source penetration testing models of similar size and even larger state-of-the-art models like Llama 3 70B and Qwen1.5 72B Chat, particularly on insane difficulty machine setups. This demonstrates that the current capabilities of general large language models (LLMs) are insufficient for effectively guiding users through the penetration testing process. We also discuss the potential for improvement through scaling and the development of better benchmarks using FARR Flow augmentation results. © 2024 by the authors.

[R0469] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 22nd International Conference on Distributed Computing in Smart Systems and the Internet of Things, DCOSS-IoT 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: An Organization-Scoped LLM Agent Runtime Architecture for Regulated Cybersecurity Operations
KEYWORDS: agent runtime; architecture; auditability; cyberaid; cybersecurity; LLM agents; Application programs; Computation theory; Cybersecurity; Digital twin; Knowledge management; Memory architecture; Network architecture; Network security; Observability; Security systems; Agent runtime; Auditability; Cyber security; Cyberaid; Language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Runtime architecture; Runtimes; Work-flows; Architecture
ABSTRACT: Regulated cybersecurity workflows lack a runtime substrate that enforces organization-level scope across retrieval, tool calls, memory, findings, reports, and audit while remaining model-agnostic and locally deployable. Recent large language model (LLM) agent systems report strong results on isolated cybersecurity tasks, yet they do not by themselves define an auditable platform architecture for regulated security operations centre (SOC) and compliance workflows, where a single analyst may trigger actions that bind the organization, and where the runtime must integrate with existing SIEM/XDR stacks as a primary source of context and alert-driven triggers rather than operate as a standalone analytical layer. This paper proposes an organization-scoped LLM agent runtime architecture for financial cybersecurity. The contribution is a typed Security Context that is created at every entry point - including SIEM/XDR notifications ingested as first-class triggers - and enforced at every component boundary, combined with a shared Runtime Core, logical specialist subagents, a governed Tool Adapter Layer exposing SIEM/XDR query, enrichment, and response primitives under uniform policy and audit, structured findings with evidence references, tiered human-in-the-loop (HITL) gates, and append-only audit. Model Context Protocol (MCP), extended telemetry, digital twins for pentesting, graph retrieval, and federated knowledge sharing are treated as optional extension paths rather than mandatory runtime assumptions. We describe an implementable slice as the architecture's testability surface, and we propose a falsifiable evaluation plan with metric-level pass criteria for architecture readiness, security-policy enforcement, evidence traceability, output quality, and operational observability. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0470] year=2026 | venue=WWW 2026 - Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Beyond Detection: Autonomous Anomaly Remediation for MCP Against Tool Poisoning Attacks
KEYWORDS: ai-enhanced web services; correct-while-generating; model context protocol; tool poisoning attack; Anomaly detection; Autonomous agents; Intelligent agents; Network security; Web services; Ai-enhanced web service; Anomaly detection; Correct-while-generating; Correction mechanism; Model context protocol; Model contexts; Poisoning attacks; Tool poisoning attack; Web automation; Webs services; Computational efficiency
ABSTRACT: LLM-powered agents are evolving from passive recommenders into autonomous executors, leveraging tools via the Model Context Protocol (MCP) for web automation. However, this paradigm introduces a new vulnerability: tool poisoning attacks that manipulate the MCP context can corrupt an agent's reasoning. Existing methods focus on anomaly detection and lack autonomous correction mechanisms, hindering their real-world deployment. In this paper, we introduce MCPFixGen, to the best of our knowledge, the first framework to provide automated anomaly detection and correction. We tackle three fundamental challenges: (1) when to initiate anomaly inspection with minimal overhead, (2) what features effectively characterize abnormality, and (3) how to automatically execute remedial actions. MCPFixGen achieves this through a novel multi-checkpoint rollback mechanism for anomaly management. MCPFixGen proposes a multi-checkpoint rollback anomaly management mechanism. Strategically places checkpoints at high-risk operations based on the tools-calling risk distribution. Then it identifies unanticipated tool behaviors by leveraging the correlation between attention activation values and tool calls. Finally, it introduces an attention-mask-based backtracking and correction mechanism. By masking the root cause of the anomaly, the LLM is prompted to re-infer, facilitating efficient and low-cost anomaly resolution. Our evaluation on real-world datasets demonstrates that MCPFixGen reduces interruptions caused by anomalies to below 13%, decreases the success rate of tool poisoning attacks to less than 0.3%, and significantly improves the robustness, computational efficiency, and task completion rate of web intelligence applications compared to existing approaches. © 2026 Owner/Author.

[R0471] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Data Science and Business Systems, ICDSBS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Executable Risk Assessment: A Multi-Modal Approach to Autonomous Security Operations
KEYWORDS: automated remediation; CVE prioritization; explainable AI; LLM patch synthesis; machine learning security; vulnerability management; Artificial intelligence; Automation; Network security; Remediation; Risk assessment; Safety engineering; Security systems; Automated remediation; Common vulnerabilities and exposures; Common vulnerability and exposure prioritization; Common vulnerability scoring systems; Explainable AI; LLM patch synthesis; Machine learning security; Machine-learning; Prioritization; Vulnerability management; Failure analysis
ABSTRACT: The so-called rapid proliferation of Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) has contributed greatly to the complexity of vulnerability management in contemporary software ecosystems. Whereas the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) offers standard severity scores, the use of fixed scores is not sufficient to rely on in order to effectively prioritize remediation especially in automated security environments. Recent threat intelligence means that 29 per cent of vulnerabilities are exploited before or on the day of public disclosure, which argues the need to have context-sensitive, evidence-based prioritization systems. The paper describes an independent vulnerability remediation model that combines Evidence-Based Priority Scoring (EBPS), GNN-based reachability analysis, LLM-based patch synthesis, and Explainable AI (XAI). On the March 2026 Patch Tuesday dataset (82 CVEs), the framework achieves a high-priority triage F1-score of 0.83 (vs. 0.61 with CVSS-only) and reduces patch failure rates to 4.8 percent (compared to 1520 percent), and improves developer trust scores of 2.3 4.1/5 with XAI explanations. Comparative analysis with CVSS v4.0, Qualys VMDR, Tenable.io, and CVEDrill confirms that the proposed system is a complex improvement over the prioritization accuracy, reachability analysis, explainability, and remediation automation. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0472] year=2026 | venue=Journal of Information and Knowledge Management | type=Article
TITLE: Agentic AI for Offensive Security: LLM-guided Autonomous Red Teaming in a Limited Cyber-range Environment
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; Autonomous penetration testing; cyber ranges; large language models; Metasploit; red teaming; reinforcement learning; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Decision making; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Pipelines; Agentic artificial intelligence; Autonomous penetration testing; Cybe range; Deterministics; Language model; Large language model; Metasploit; Penetration testing; Red teaming; Reinforcement learnings; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being explored for automating offensive security and red teaming tasks, enabling systems that can coordinate multi-step cyber operations through structured decision-making. While prior research has investigated reinforcement learning (RL) agents and large language models (LLMs) for penetration testing, most studies are evaluated in simulated or abstract environments, with limited empirical validation in real cyber-range settings. This paper presents a controlled experimental evaluation of an LLM-guided offensive security pipeline against deterministic scripted baselines in a cyber-range environment. Using a vulnerable Kioptrix virtual machine and a Kali Linux attacker, we implement three deterministic pipelines, fixed-path, sequential and rule-based, alongside two configurations of an LLM-guided agent: an initial version (LLM V1) and a refined constrained controller (LLM V2). All approaches operate within a restricted and auditable action space executed through predefined tools. Across repeated trials, the initial LLM configuration exhibits reduced reliability and increased execution cost due to exploratory behaviour. In contrast, the refined controller achieves a 100% success rate, reduces execution steps, eliminates wasted actions and matches the efficiency of rule-based automation. These results show that, within a controlled cyber-range environment, LLM-guided agents can approximate deterministic performance when appropriate constraints are applied. This suggests that agent-based approaches to offensive security can support semi-autonomous red teaming workflows, provided that decision-making is governed by structured control policies. © 2026 World Scientific Publishing Co.

[R0473] year=2025 | venue=2025 Cyber Awareness and Research Symposium, CARS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Multi-agent Cyber Defense: A Novel Approach Using Reinforcement Learning with Hierarchical LLM Critics
KEYWORDS: Cyber Defense; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Systems; Network Security; Reinforcement Learning; Autonomous agents; Deep learning; Hierarchical systems; Intelligent agents; Network security; Semantics; Cyber threats; Cyber-defense; Enterprise networks; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Networks security; Organizational policies; Reinforcement learnings; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Modern enterprise networks are continuously ex-panding in both scale and complexity. Alongside this, cyber threats have become more dangerous and dynamic. Consequently, automating cyber tasks by creating AI agents is essential for effectively countering these evolving threats. Reinforcement learning (RL) and Deep Learning (DL) models have shown promise in this area, but suffer from a high false-positive rate, slow convergence, and a lack of context-aware strategies. Incorporation of cyber domain knowledge (threat reports, attack behavior descriptions, LLMs trained on cyber data, etc.) might enable these agents to make informed decisions. In this work, we propose a novel multi-agent architecture to enhance RL-based autonomous agents using a hierarchy of large language models (LLMs). Our proposed approach enables real-time adaptation to new attack patterns, potentially without retraining the LLMs. We discuss the use of prompt engineering (to encode organizational policies and shape agent behavior) and retrieval augmented generation to facilitate communication between LLMs and ensure actions are aligned with organizational policies. Our approach aims to bridge semantic understanding with strategic RL-agentic control, offering a scalable and modular solution for autonomous multi-agent cyber defense. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0474] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - IEEE Military Communications Conference MILCOM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic AI for Cyber Defense: LLM-Guided Hierarchical Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cybersecurity; Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning; Complex networks; Computational methods; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Dynamic network environment; Hierarchical reinforcement learning; Intrinsic rewards; Language model; Large language model; Multi-agent reinforcement learning; Multiagent teams; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: In cyber defense, agentic AI in the form of distributed multi-agent teams must rapidly adapt to evolving threats across dynamic network environments. Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HRL) mitigates sample complexity by decomposing complex objectives into simpler subskills, yet handcrafting these skills and their intrinsic rewards remains expertise-dependent and resource-intensive. We introduce a novel agentic AI framework that harnesses the task decomposition and planning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate both skill discovery and reward shaping within HRL. By prompting an LLM to identify semantically meaningful skills and craft corresponding intrinsic reward functions, our method significantly reduces manual engineering effort. We validate our approach in CAGE Challenge 4, a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) cybersecurity environment, showing that our LLM-guided hierarchical policy achieves faster convergence and improved performance in cyber defense relative to flat MARL baselines. These results underscore the potential of integrating agentic AI into multi-agent HRL for scalable, interpretable, and efficient cyber-defense. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0475] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: From AI Strategy to Executable Controls: A PRISMA-Guided Systematic Review of AI Governance for Cybersecurity Efficiency in Azerbaijan
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; AI governance; Azerbaijan; control taxonomy; cybersecurity; LLM security; MLOps; MTTD; MTTR; PRISMA 2020; SIEM; XAI; Computer privacy; Critical infrastructures; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; Life cycle; National security; Prisms; Public policy; Public works; Agentic AI; AI governance; Azerbaijan; Control taxonomy; Cyber security; LLM security; MLOp; MTTD; MTTR; PRISMA 2020; SIEM; XAI; Efficiency
ABSTRACT: The deployment of artificial intelligence across public-sector services, critical infrastructure, and national security operations creates an urgent demand for governance systems that can translate strategic principles into auditable, executable technical controls. This paper reports a completed PRISMA 2020 systematic literature review (n = 38; inter-rater reliability κ = 0.83) and derives from it a nine-pillar national AI governance control taxonomy tailored to Azerbaijan's legislative context. Each pillar is grounded in peer-reviewed evidence spanning responsible AI ethics, LLM security, MLOps governance, privacy-preserving computing, explainability, continuous monitoring, and agentic AI safety. All nine pillars are mapped to three operative national policy instruments - the AI Development Strategy 2025-2028, the Information Security and Cybersecurity Strategy 2023-2027, and the Law on Protection of Critical Information Infrastructure No. 949 - at the article level. Concrete technical implementation artefacts are proposed for each pillar together with five measurable operational efficiency KPIs, including Mean Time to Detect (MTTD ≤ 24 h) and Mean Time to Respond (MTTR ≤ 72 h), calibrated against CISA, ENISA, and NIST baselines and adjusted to reflect Azerbaijan's current SOC maturity and CII enforcement capacity. Cross-pillar dependency analysis and a structural comparison with the EU AI Act are also provided. The framework addresses a documented gap in post-Soviet AI governance scholarship by offering a replicable, evidence-based pathway from national AI strategy to operational cybersecurity control. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0476] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Adaptive Ticket Routing through Retrieval-Augmented In-Context Learning from Analyst Behaviour at CERT-EU
KEYWORDS: continual adaptation; human-in-the-loop; in-context learning; retrieval-augmented classification; security operations; Artificial intelligence; Behavioral research; Information systems; Learning systems; Metadata; Network security; Queueing theory; Context learning; Continual adaptation; Human-in-the-loop; In contexts; In-context learning; Operational tasks; Retrieval-augmented classification; Routing policies; Routings; Security operations; Demonstrations
ABSTRACT: Ticket routing is a critical operational task: organisations must triage incoming requests to specialised teams, typically via queues, but routing policies are organisation-specific and evolve continuously. Rule-based systems demand constant maintenance, while supervised classifiers suffer from concept drift and require periodic retraining. These challenges intensify in regulated environments where sensitive data prevents cloud-based AI services, annotation capacity is limited, and misrouting carries significant cost. We present an adaptive ticket routing system that frames routing as retrieval-augmented in-context learning with temporal feedback learning. Rather than training a static model, the system retrieves similar successfully routed tickets as demonstrations, grounding predictions in accumulated organisational expertise. It learns continuously from routine analyst behaviour: corrections are captured as error signals, while predictions unchanged beyond a calibrated time threshold are interpreted as implicit confirmation - -transforming everyday workflow into supervision at zero incremental annotation cost. Predictions are constrained to queues present in retrieved demonstrations; an on-premises LLM resolves conflicts only when demonstrations disagree. Deployed at CERT-EU (cybersecurity service provider for European Union entities), the system operates in two modes: assisted routing, where analysts receive recommendations based on similar historical cases (2× speedup); and selective autonomy, where tickets assigned to high-confidence queues are routed automatically (22× speedup). Over six months of production deployment (1900 tickets), it achieves 86.3% weighted F1, outperforming supervised baselines by 4.5 points and zero-shot LLM inference by 15 points. The system runs entirely on-premises and adapts continuously without retraining or dedicated annotation, offering a practical blueprint for organisations operating under strict data-sovereignty constraints. © 2026 Owner/Author.


